//@version=5
// =====================================================================================
//  VSISA SETUPS - the three-stage pattern, drawn on the chart
//
//  Zee, 2026-09-19: "let's create a drawing on a tradingview indicator of our setups..
//  what if we make the heavy volume as the heaviest of the last"
//
//  Counted on 28 sessions of OANDA M5, the funnel runs:
//      runs of 2+ one way                  60.68 / day
//      ...heavy volume (campaign)          18.29   -70%
//      ...then an opposite bar             18.25    -0%
//      ...low volume OR wick               11.21   -39%
//      ...then continues on light volume    4.36   -61%
//
//  TWO WAYS TO CALL A CAMPAIGN HEAVY, because they are not the same test:
//    AVERAGE  - the run averages >= x the last N bars' average volume. Loose; this is
//               what the counting script used and it gave 18.29 campaigns a day.
//    PEAK     - the run's loudest bar reaches >= x the loudest bar of the last N. This
//               is what VSISA the EA actually demands (0.70 x the swing peak) and it is
//               far stricter - the likeliest reason the EA fires 0.80 times a day
//               against the pattern's 4.36.
//  Switch between them and see which marks the setups you would really take.
//
//  THE WICK IS AN ALTERNATIVE, NOT AN EXTRA. A reaction qualifies on LOW VOLUME or on a
//  REJECTION WICK - measured on OANDA data, the wick nearly doubles the qualifying
//  reactions (148 -> 284), because absorption inside a loud bar is the same information
//  as a quiet bar, arriving differently.
//
//  Volume colour follows TRADINGVIEW's convention throughout: a bar is buying when
//  close > open. Never MT5's "smaller than the previous bar".
// =====================================================================================
indicator("VSISA Setups", overlay = true, max_labels_count = 500, max_boxes_count = 500, max_lines_count = 500)

// ---- inputs -------------------------------------------------------------------------
grpC   = "Campaign"
minRun = input.int(2,    "Campaign: minimum bars in the run", minval = 1, group = grpC)
maxRun = input.int(8,    "Campaign: most bars to count back", minval = 2, group = grpC)
method = input.string("PEAK (like the EA)", "Heavy volume measured against", options = ["AVERAGE of last N", "PEAK (like the EA)"], group = grpC)
lookN  = input.int(20,   "N bars for that yardstick", minval = 5, group = grpC)
avgX   = input.float(1.15, "AVERAGE method: run avg >= x average", step = 0.05, group = grpC)
pkX    = input.float(0.70, "PEAK method: run's loudest >= x peak",  step = 0.05, group = grpC)

grpR   = "Reaction"
//  WHAT IS "LOW VOLUME" LOW AGAINST? Zee, 2026-09-19, after a setup he would have taken
//  went unmarked: "i think we compare the reaction candle's volume to the LAST CANDLE of
//  the campaign (the candle before the reaction candle is the last candle of the
//  campaign)". His law says only "then we have a low volume bullish candle" (line 223)
//  and leaves the yardstick open, so this was my choice, not his - and mine was wrong for
//  the bar he was looking at. The 18 Sep 12:50 reaction came in at 2,313 against a
//  campaign AVERAGING 2,162 (107% - failed) but against its LAST bar of 3,280 it is 71%,
//  which passes comfortably. His eye was comparing it to the big red right before it.
quietRef = input.string("LAST bar of the campaign", "Low volume measured against", options = ["LAST bar of the campaign", "campaign AVERAGE", "campaign PEAK"], group = grpR)
relX   = input.float(0.85, "Low volume: reaction <= x that reference", step = 0.05, group = grpR)
//  Zee, 2026-09-19: "if volume of reaction candle is considerably shorter than the
//  campaign's last candle's volume we can say on the label this too: strong imbalance".
//  That is the ideal case in his own notes, line 98: "ideal case: the reaction candle
//  would've been LOW VOLUME while coming down -> means IMBALANCE of demand supply -> means
//  demand has reduced -> LIQUIDITY PROVIDERS HAVE REMOVED ORDERS". Merely clearing 0.85
//  is qualifying; collapsing to half is the other side walking away.
imbX   = input.float(0.55, "STRONG imbalance: reaction <= x that reference", step = 0.05, group = grpR)
wickX  = input.float(0.25, "OR rejection wick >= x of the bar range", step = 0.05, group = grpR)
aggWickX = input.float(0.10, "Call it aggressive buying/selling past x", step = 0.05, group = grpR)
//  LAWS_VSISA.md line 125: "it broke below the main bullish candle (ENGULFED THE BODY of
//  previous candle)". Zee, 2026-09-19: "we can also check if the candle body envelopes
//  (engulfs) the last two campaign candle's bodies, we can add to the label Stronger:
//  engulfs last 2". The reaction taking back two or three whole bodies in one bar is the
//  campaign being undone faster than it was built.
showEngulf = input.bool(true, "Count campaign bodies the reaction engulfs", group = grpR)
useWick= input.bool(true,  "Let a wick qualify a LOUD reaction", group = grpR)

grpE   = "End of rising / falling market"
//  LAWS_VSISA.md line 249-250: "End of rising market: large blue bar with SMALL volume,
//  means there's an imbalance, supply is less, so small volume moved large spread.. then
//  SMALL blue bar even though HIGH volume.. in a bullish trend.. means supply hit.. and
//  its reaction bearish.. that I call end of rising market.. sell setup"
//  Line 258: "end of rising scenario.. as small blue candle has a large volume"
//
//  WHY IT MATTERS, line 111-113 - it is a QUALIFIER, not decoration:
//    "IF no small candle big volume previously AND not wick on top of the red reaction
//     candle -> WE WOULD NOT SELL IT"
//    "but because small blue candle has big volume (end of rising market type) + next bar
//     with wick on top -> this big red volume is of AGGRESSIVE SELLING and not of demand"
//  So it does the same job as the wick: it is what makes a LOUD reaction tradeable.
//  Without either, the law stands down. Mirrored as END OF FALLING for the buy side.
showEOR  = input.bool(true,  "Mark end of rising / falling market", group = grpE)
eorSpread= input.float(0.70, "Its candle's spread <= x average range", step = 0.05, group = grpE)
eorVol   = input.float(1.15, "...while its volume >= x average volume", step = 0.05, group = grpE)
eorPrev  = input.float(1.10, "The bar before it: spread >= x average", step = 0.05, group = grpE)
eorLook  = input.int(3,      "Look back this many bars from the reaction", minval = 1, group = grpE)
//  Zee, 2026-09-20: "if campaign's last candle is very small BODIED, and has a very large
//  volume, and its reaction (next bar) is bearish.. label it end of rising market.. sell
//  setup". That is line 250 word for word: "if choti c candle pe bohot bara volume ayay,
//  means supply hit, aur usska reaction bearish ho, next bar down jaey, ussko END OF
//  RISING MARKET bolta hu.. SELL SETUP".
//  The sequence only completes when the NEXT bar turns - so this is checked at the
//  reaction, not at the capped bar, and it renames the whole setup rather than adding a
//  footnote. Body is measured as well as spread because he said "bodied": a pin bar can
//  have a wide range and still be a tiny body, and that is capping too.
eorBody  = input.float(0.35, "...or its BODY <= x average range", step = 0.05, group = grpE)

grpL   = "Last campaign candle"
//  LAWS_VSISA.md line 279: "if there was a WICK BELOW the red candle, then the bigger
//  volume would mean its DEMAND". And line 35: "if our red volume candles are breaking
//  down below the support line WITH A WICK and closing above the support line, its a
//  strong sign."
//  So the campaign's own last candle can give the tell BEFORE the reaction prints: a red
//  bar pushed back off its low means buyers were already arriving inside it. Mirrored for
//  a buying campaign - an upper wick on its last blue candle means sellers were there.
showLast = input.bool(true,  "Label a wick on the campaign's last candle", group = grpL)
lastWick = input.float(0.25, "That wick >= x of its range", step = 0.05, group = grpL)

grpF   = "Support / fake break"
//  LAWS_VSISA.md line 35: "if we draw a line called SUPPORT below the LAST LOW before the
//  reaction candle, then if our red volume candles are BREAKING DOWN BELOW the support
//  line WITH A WICK and CLOSING ABOVE the support line, its a strong sign."
//  Line 234: "2 bar setup + FAKE BREAK of this support + forms a wick + closing again
//  inside support (STRONGEST)". Line 243: "previous low (support) is broken by a pinbar
//  (wick).. strong move because fake break of support happened with a wick."
//  The support is the last swing low before the campaign - taken here as the most recent
//  confirmed pivot, which is what "the last low point" means on a chart.
showSup   = input.bool(true, "Draw support / resistance and mark fake breaks", group = grpF)
pivLeft   = input.int(3, "Bars each side that define the pivot", minval = 1, group = grpF)
pivRight  = input.int(3, "...bars to the right (the confirmation lag)", minval = 1, group = grpF)

grpS   = "Strong closing"
//  LAWS_VSISA.md line 277: "the red candle's closing is near the low (NEAR ZERO LOWER
//  WICK)". Line 125: "top wick on reaction candle + STRONG BEARISH CLOSING (on the low the
//  closing of candle happened) + it broke below the main bullish candle.. so this could be
//  taken as AGGRESSIVE SELLING instead of demand".
//  And the full definition, line 156: "aggressive selling: supply has hit + has a wick +
//  closing strong bearish".
//
//  Note it is the OPPOSITE wick from the rejection one. A sell wants a wick ON TOP
//  (sellers hit it) AND almost nothing below (they held it down into the close). The two
//  together are what separate aggressive selling from demand.
showClose  = input.bool(true, "Mark strong bearish / bullish closings", group = grpS)
closeMax   = input.float(0.12, "Closing wick <= x of the bar range", step = 0.02, group = grpS)
//  THE WICK ON THE WRONG SIDE. Zee, 2026-09-20, on an END OF RISING MARKET - SELL SETUP
//  that was followed by price rising: "the same candle has a wick at the bottom of the
//  candle.. market goes upwards after it.. could our wick laws have classified it here as
//  weak or something?"
//  Yes, and line 279 is explicit: "if there was a WICK BELOW the red candle, then the
//  bigger volume would mean its DEMAND". I built that rule for the campaign's last candle
//  and never for the REACTION itself. That bar had a 21% wick above and a 16% wick below -
//  nearly symmetric, which is indecision rather than rejection - and price went +6.21 over
//  the next four bars. A sell worth taking wants a wick above and almost nothing below.
oppWickX   = input.float(0.15, "A wick on the WRONG side past x weakens the setup", step = 0.05, group = grpS)

grpX   = "Confirmation"
//  OFF by default, 2026-09-19. The confirmation bar costs 61% of the setups - 11.21
//  reactions a day become 4.36 - and nobody has measured whether the ones it throws away
//  would have worked. Zee chose to see them all and judge himself, which is the same
//  division of labour as the meter: the machine finds them, he decides.
needCont = input.bool(false, "Require the next bar to continue on light volume", group = grpX)

grpW   = "Why not?"
//  Zee, 2026-09-20: "i still dont see the 12:10 setup labelled". Rather than keep
//  diagnosing from my copy of the OANDA table - which may not be the bar he is looking at
//  at all - this puts the verdict on HIS chart. Every bar that turns against a run of
//  minRun or more, but does not become a setup, says which gate refused it and with what
//  numbers. A disagreement then points at a specific figure instead of a mystery.
showWhy = input.bool(false, "Explain every bar that ALMOST qualified", group = grpW)

grpD   = "Drawing"
showRun  = input.bool(false, "Arrow every bar in a 2+ run", group = grpD)
showAll  = input.bool(true, "Mark reactions that never confirmed (only when confirmation is ON)", group = grpD)
showTbl  = input.bool(true, "Show the counter", group = grpD)

// ---- the bar's own direction, TradingView convention --------------------------------
dir = close > open ? 1 : close < open ? -1 : 0

// ---- find the run that ENDED on the previous bar -------------------------------------
// this bar goes the other way, so the run behind it is a candidate campaign
runDir  = dir[1]
runLen  = 0
runVol  = 0.0
runPeak = 0.0
if dir != 0 and runDir != 0 and dir != runDir
    for k = 1 to maxRun
        d = close[k] > open[k] ? 1 : close[k] < open[k] ? -1 : 0
        if d != runDir
            break
        runLen  += 1
        runVol  += volume[k]
        runPeak := math.max(runPeak, volume[k])

campAvg = runLen > 0 ? runVol / runLen : 0.0

// ---- WAS THE CAMPAIGN'S LAST CANDLE CAPPED? --------------------------------------------
//  Computed HERE, before the reaction is judged, because it decides whether a LOUD
//  reaction is allowed at all. Zee, 2026-09-20: "please note that here the reaction
//  candle's volume CAN BE LARGER than the campaign's last volume.. its the ONLY case where
//  we can allow a larger volume (instead of the usual lower volume case)".
//  Line 250 spells out the negative: "the red reaction candle has a large volume, IF
//  previous candles were NOT end of rising market.. then we would be CAUTIOUS that there's
//  some demand.. if there were no wick on top of the red reaction candle then we would say
//  we have a demand still WE WOULD WAIT."
avgRange = ta.sma(high - low, lookN)
volSma   = ta.sma(volume, lookN)
prevCapped = ((high[1] - low[1]) <= avgRange[1] * eorSpread or math.abs(close[1] - open[1]) <= avgRange[1] * eorBody) and volume[1] >= volSma[1] * eorVol

//  ...AND ANYWHERE ELSE IN THE CAMPAIGN, not only on its last candle.
//  Zee, 2026-09-20: "but the second last bar before the 12:10 AM bar was capping.. can u
//  check?" He was right and the rule was too narrow. On 18 Sep 22:10 the campaign ran
//  21:55 / 22:00 / 22:05, and 21:55 is capped on the script's own thresholds - spread 0.67
//  of average on 1.31x volume - but sat three bars back where nothing looked. The supply
//  that caps a rise does not have to land on the very last candle before the turn.
//  prevCapped is kept separately because line 250's end-of-rising SEQUENCE specifically
//  needs the capped bar to be the last one, with the reaction right after it.
//  The best candidate is remembered even when it fails, so the Why-not label can show how
//  close it came. On 18 Sep 22:10 the capping bar cleared the volume test by ONE PERCENT
//  in my own check and landed just under it on the chart, because Pine's rolling average
//  covers a slightly different window - a knife-edge that looked like a flat "no".
cappedRun = false
capBestSpread = 99.0
capBestBody   = 99.0
capBestVol    = 0.0
if runLen > 0
    for k = 1 to runLen
        sR = avgRange[k] > 0 ? (high[k] - low[k]) / avgRange[k] : 99.0
        bR = avgRange[k] > 0 ? math.abs(close[k] - open[k]) / avgRange[k] : 99.0
        vR = volSma[k] > 0 ? volume[k] / volSma[k] : 0.0
        if (sR <= eorSpread or bR <= eorBody) and vR >= eorVol
            cappedRun := true
        if math.min(sR, bR) < math.min(capBestSpread, capBestBody)
            capBestSpread := sR
            capBestBody   := bR
            capBestVol    := vR

// ---- is it heavy? ---------------------------------------------------------------------
volAvgN  = ta.sma(volume, lookN)[1]
volPeakN = ta.highest(volume, lookN)[1]
heavy = method == "AVERAGE of last N" ? campAvg >= volAvgN * avgX : runPeak >= volPeakN * pkX
isCampaign = runLen >= minRun and heavy and campAvg > 0

// ---- the reaction: THIS bar, opposite to the campaign ---------------------------------
rng      = high - low
lowWick  = math.min(open, close) - low
highWick = high - math.max(open, close)
wick     = runDir < 0 ? lowWick : highWick          // rejection against the campaign
quietBase = quietRef == "LAST bar of the campaign" ? volume[1] : quietRef == "campaign PEAK" ? runPeak : campAvg
quiet    = quietBase > 0 and volume <= quietBase * relX
strongImb = quietBase > 0 and volume <= quietBase * imbX
wicked   = useWick and rng > 0 and (wick / rng) >= wickX

//  TWO WAYS IN, AND THEY ARE NOT EQUAL.
//    QUIET  - the usual case. Low volume qualifies on its own; that IS the imbalance.
//    LOUD   - allowed ONLY with the capping AND the wick together. This is the single
//             exception where a reaction may carry as much volume as the campaign's last
//             bar, or more. Without both, line 250 says "we have a demand still we would
//             WAIT", and line 111 "we would not sell it".
//  I previously let ANY wicked reaction qualify whatever its volume, which admitted loud
//  reactions with nothing capping them - exactly the demand case the law refuses.
loudOK   = wicked and cappedRun
absorbed = isCampaign and (quiet or loudOK)

// ---- confirmation arrives on the NEXT bar ---------------------------------------------
contDir  = -runDir                                   // a selling campaign sets up a BUY
contUp    = contDir[1] > 0 ? close > open : close < open
confirmed = absorbed[1] and needCont and contUp and volume <= campAvg[1]
fired = needCont ? confirmed : absorbed

// ---- the run ending ON THIS BAR, so it can be marked live rather than in hindsight ----
runNow  = 0
peakNow = 0.0
volNow  = 0.0
if dir != 0
    for k = 0 to maxRun - 1
        d = close[k] > open[k] ? 1 : close[k] < open[k] ? -1 : 0
        if d != dir
            break
        runNow  += 1
        volNow  += volume[k]
        peakNow := math.max(peakNow, volume[k])
avgNow   = runNow > 0 ? volNow / runNow : 0.0
smaNow  = ta.sma(volume, lookN)
hiNow   = ta.highest(volume, lookN)
heavyNow = method == "AVERAGE of last N" ? avgNow >= smaNow[runNow] * avgX : peakNow >= hiNow[runNow] * pkX
inRunNow = runNow >= minRun
campNow  = inRunNow and heavyNow

// ---- drawing ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// NO barcolor() HERE. Zee: "can u remove all colors from the candle bodies.. to retain
// original candle colors". The candles keep TradingView's own colouring; the campaign is
// already carried by the background shade and by the volume pane, so repainting the bodies
// was a third copy of the same fact and it buried the price action underneath it.

plotshape(showRun and inRunNow and dir < 0, title = "in a selling run", style = shape.triangledown, location = location.abovebar, size = size.tiny, color = color.new(color.red, 20))
plotshape(showRun and inRunNow and dir > 0, title = "in a buying run", style = shape.triangleup, location = location.belowbar, size = size.tiny, color = color.new(color.teal, 20))

// NO bgcolor() EITHER. Zee: "can you remove the background vertical lines from the
// chart". With the candle bodies already left alone, the price chart now carries only
// WORDS - the campaign is read in the volume pane, where it is a fact about volume.

// WORDS, NOT SYMBOLS. Zee: "instead of diamonds can u write labels.. because its hard to
// track so many colors / diamonds etc". A shape has to be remembered; a word does not.
if showAll and absorbed and needCont
    rLab = quiet ? "reaction: QUIET" : "reaction: WICK"
    rCol = quiet ? color.new(color.yellow, 15) : color.new(color.orange, 15)
    rSty = runDir < 0 ? label.style_label_up : label.style_label_down
    label.new(bar_index, runDir < 0 ? low : high, rLab, style = rSty, color = rCol, textcolor = color.black, size = size.small)

// ---- SUPPORT / RESISTANCE, AND THE FAKE BREAK -------------------------------------------
//  The level is the last confirmed swing low (for a selling campaign) or swing high (for a
//  buying one) - "the horizontal line coming from the last low point", as Zee put it.
//  A FAKE BREAK is that level pierced by a WICK and taken back before the close: line 35's
//  "breaking down below the support line with a wick and CLOSING ABOVE the support line".
//  It is checked on the campaign's LAST candle, which is where he asked for it, and again
//  on the reaction itself - line 234 calls that combination the strongest case there is.
var float supLvl = na
var float resLvl = na
pl = ta.pivotlow(low, pivLeft, pivRight)
ph = ta.pivothigh(high, pivLeft, pivRight)
if not na(pl)
    supLvl := pl
if not na(ph)
    resLvl := ph

lvl = runDir < 0 ? supLvl : resLvl
//  pierced and recovered - on the campaign's last bar
fakeLast = not na(lvl) and (runDir < 0 ? (low[1] < lvl and close[1] > lvl) : (high[1] > lvl and close[1] < lvl))
//  ...and on the reaction bar itself
fakeReact = not na(lvl) and (runDir < 0 ? (low < lvl and close > lvl) : (high > lvl and close < lvl))
fakeBreak = fakeLast or fakeReact

if showSup and isCampaign and fakeLast
    fTxt = runDir < 0 ? "FAKE BREAK of support" : "FAKE BREAK of resistance"
    fSub = runDir < 0 ? "wicked below " + str.tostring(lvl, format.mintick) + " and closed back above" : "wicked above " + str.tostring(lvl, format.mintick) + " and closed back below"
    fCol = color.new(color.purple, 15)
    fSty = runDir < 0 ? label.style_label_up : label.style_label_down
    label.new(bar_index - 1, runDir < 0 ? low[1] : high[1], fTxt + "\n" + fSub, style = fSty, color = fCol, textcolor = color.white, size = size.small)

if showSup and isCampaign and not na(lvl)
    line.new(bar_index - runLen - 2, lvl, bar_index + 2, lvl, color = color.new(color.purple, 40), style = line.style_dashed, width = 1)

// ---- THE CAMPAIGN'S LAST CANDLE ---------------------------------------------------------
//  Zee, 2026-09-19: "we can label the last candle of campaign if it has a large lower wick
//  on its closing, it should say: lower wick suggests buying".
//  That is line 279 - "if there was a wick below the red candle, then the bigger volume
//  would mean its DEMAND" - and it is the earliest tell in the whole sequence: the other
//  side showing up INSIDE the campaign, one bar before the reaction prints.
lastRng   = high[1] - low[1]
lastLow   = math.min(open[1], close[1]) - low[1]
lastHigh  = high[1] - math.max(open[1], close[1])
lastWickV = runDir < 0 ? lastLow : lastHigh
lastHas   = lastRng > 0 and (lastWickV / lastRng) >= lastWick

if showLast and isCampaign and lastHas
    lTxt = runDir < 0 ? "lower wick suggests buying" : "upper wick suggests selling"
    lSub = runDir < 0 ? "pushed off its low on the last campaign bar - demand already there" : "pushed off its high on the last campaign bar - supply already there"
    lCol = runDir < 0 ? color.new(color.teal, 30) : color.new(color.red, 30)
    lSty = runDir < 0 ? label.style_label_up : label.style_label_down
    label.new(bar_index - 1, runDir < 0 ? low[1] : high[1], lTxt + "\n" + lSub, style = lSty, color = lCol, textcolor = color.white, size = size.small)

// ---- END OF RISING / FALLING MARKET ---------------------------------------------------
//  The signature is effort without result at the END of a move: a candle whose spread is
//  SMALL while its volume is LARGE, in the direction the market has been going. Line 88:
//  "small blue with a large volume -> supply was hit -> this small candle means aggressive
//  selling happened which didnot let the price move".
//  The ideal adds the bar before it - large spread on small volume, line 254-255 - which
//  is the imbalance that precedes the cap. Line 90 allows the relaxed version: "if small
//  candle big volume and previously bullish candle and its reaction is bearish.. then its
//  not a perfect end of rising but still its like end of rising market".
//  avgRange, volSma and prevCapped are declared further up, next to the campaign, because
//  the reaction's own test needs them before this point.
smallBar = (high - low) <= avgRange * eorSpread
loudBar  = volume >= volSma * eorVol
wideQuietBefore = (high[1] - low[1]) >= avgRange[1] * eorPrev and volume[1] <= volSma[1]

smallBody = math.abs(close - open) <= avgRange * eorBody
cappedBar = (smallBar or smallBody) and loudBar

eorUp   = dir > 0 and cappedBar and dir[1] > 0        // end of RISING  -> sell
eorDown = dir < 0 and cappedBar and dir[1] < 0        // end of FALLING -> buy
eorIdeal = wideQuietBefore

//  THE COMPLETED SEQUENCE - the capped bar is the campaign's LAST candle and THIS bar is
//  the reaction that turns. Line 250 only calls it end of rising market once the next bar
//  goes the other way, so it is judged here rather than on the capped bar itself.
eorSellNow = isCampaign and runDir > 0 and prevCapped and dir < 0
eorBuyNow  = isCampaign and runDir < 0 and prevCapped and dir > 0
eorNow     = eorSellNow or eorBuyNow

// did one happen within reach of this reaction?
eorNearUp = false
eorNearDn = false
for k = 1 to eorLook
    eorNearUp := eorNearUp or eorUp[k]
    eorNearDn := eorNearDn or eorDown[k]

if showEOR and (eorUp or eorDown)
    //  PRICE CAPPED is Zee's wording for this bar and it is the clearer one - 2026-09-19:
    //  "if during campaign we notice a very small red candle with a large volume.. it
    //  means its capping.. supply is being hit and the buy orders are not letting it fall
    //  down further.. can we label this baby candle as price capped".
    //  It is the SAME measurement as end of rising/falling - line 53: "small candle with
    //  big volume shows that big demand came that CAPS THE MARKET and not letting the
    //  market going down" - so it gets one label, not two. The end-of-market name is kept
    //  for the full pattern, where the wide quiet bar before it makes it the terminal case
    //  rather than a pause mid-campaign.
    eLab = eorIdeal ? (eorUp ? "PRICE CAPPED - END OF RISING MARKET" : "PRICE CAPPED - END OF FALLING MARKET") : "PRICE CAPPED"
    eSub = eorUp ? "small blue candle on big volume - supply hit, the rise is being capped" : "small red candle on big volume - demand hit, the fall is being capped"
    eCol = eorUp ? color.new(color.maroon, 10) : color.new(color.green, 10)
    eSty = eorUp ? label.style_label_down : label.style_label_up
    label.new(bar_index, eorUp ? high : low, eLab + "\n" + eSub, style = eSty, color = eCol, textcolor = color.white, size = size.small)

buySig  = fired and (needCont ? contDir[1] > 0 : contDir > 0)
sellSig = fired and (needCont ? contDir[1] < 0 : contDir < 0)

// --- THE WICK WARNING, straight out of LAWS_VSISA.md line 219:
//  "now if this blue candle has NO WICK below it .. then it means supply is being hit..
//   we wouldn't buy.. we hold .. a wick below means aggressive buying happened"
//  and line 243: "bullish candle with a wick below it.. if there was no wick we'd say
//  there's still supply".
//  So the wick is the GOOD sign - buy orders turned the candle - and its ABSENCE is the
//  caution. Zee asked for the warning on the wick; his own law puts it on the missing
//  one, and the law wins. Mirrored for sells: a red reaction wants a wick on TOP, and
//  without one "we would say we have a demand still we would wait" (line 250).
//  The setup still prints - this only marks it as the weaker version.
//  SAY THE MEASUREMENT, NOT A VERDICT. Zee: "this red candle is QUIET. it has large wick
//  on top and also on below. but it has a label saying no wick". The detection was right
//  but the wording was not: the test is wick / RANGE >= 25%, so on a tall candle with
//  wicks both sides and a big body, a wick that looks large can still be under a quarter
//  of the whole bar. "no wick" claimed something false about the candle. It now prints
//  the number, so a disagreement is a threshold to argue with rather than a puzzle.
//  Which wick counts depends on the side: a BUY wants it BELOW, a SELL wants it ABOVE.
hasWick = rng > 0 and (wick / rng) >= wickX
wickPct = rng > 0 ? wick / rng * 100 : 0

// ---- HOW MANY CAMPAIGN BODIES DID THE REACTION SWALLOW? --------------------------------
//  Bodies only, not wicks - line 125 says "engulfed the BODY of previous candle". Counted
//  backwards from the nearest campaign bar and stopping at the first one it does not
//  cover, so "engulfs last 3" really means the three immediately behind it.
bodyTop = math.max(open, close)
bodyBot = math.min(open, close)
engulfN = 0
if showEngulf and runLen > 0
    for k = 1 to runLen
        if bodyTop >= math.max(open[k], close[k]) and bodyBot <= math.min(open[k], close[k])
            engulfN += 1
        else
            break

//  AND TRANSLATE IT. Zee: "i appreciate the percentages.. but then translate it too.. so
//  say BUY SETUP lower wick 38% means XXX". The number is the evidence; this is what the
//  law reads INTO it. For a buy, the wick below is how far sellers drove it before buy
//  orders took it back - line 243: "wick below says buy order was hit.. if there was no
//  wick this could be a supply". For a sell it is the same story upside down, line 250:
//  "the wick tells that when market was trying to go up there was a large sell order".
//  NO TIERS. Zee, 2026-09-19: "we don't need tiers.. infact any existance of lower wick
//  greater than 10% is enough to classify it". His law never graded it either - line 219
//  says flatly "a wick below means AGGRESSIVE BUYING happened", line 243 "the wick suggests
//  the big volume is due to aggressive buying". My 25/45 bands were an invention on top of
//  a document that makes a binary call, so they are gone: past 10% it is aggressive buying,
//  and 45% only intensifies the same verdict rather than changing it.
wickMeaning = ""
if wickPct >= 45
    wickMeaning := buySig ? "STRONG aggressive buying" : "STRONG aggressive selling"
else if wickPct >= aggWickX * 100
    wickMeaning := buySig ? "aggressive buying" : "aggressive selling"
else
    wickMeaning := buySig ? "nothing pushed back - supply still available, the law says HOLD" : "nothing pushed back - demand still there, the law says WAIT"

//  THE TWO WAYS A LOUD REACTION BECOMES TRADEABLE (line 111-113). Either a wick, or an
//  end-of-rising/falling before it. With neither, the law does not take it - so the
//  warning only stands when BOTH are missing, and the end-of-market note is shown when it
//  is what rescued the setup.
eorHere = buySig ? eorNearDn : eorNearUp

// ---- STRONG CLOSING -------------------------------------------------------------------
//  The wick on the CLOSING side, which the law wants to be nearly absent: a red candle
//  closing on its low, a blue candle closing on its high. That is the side holding what it
//  took, rather than giving it back before the bell.
closeWick = dir < 0 ? lowWick : highWick
closePct  = rng > 0 ? closeWick / rng * 100 : 100
strongClose = rng > 0 and (closeWick / rng) <= closeMax

if showClose and strongClose and (buySig or sellSig)
    //  WORDING. "sellers held every inch" was flavour, not information. The question the
    //  law is actually settling here (lines 110-113) is whether that big volume was
    //  AGGRESSIVE SELLING or DEMAND - so the line says which, and shows the measurement
    //  that decided it rather than a figure of speech.
    cTxt = dir < 0 ? "strong bearish closing" : "strong bullish closing"
    cSub = dir < 0 ? "means selling pressure, no buyers" : "means buying pressure, no sellers"
    cCol = dir < 0 ? color.new(color.red, 25) : color.new(color.teal, 25)
    cSty = dir < 0 ? label.style_label_left : label.style_label_left
    label.new(bar_index, close, cTxt + "\n" + cSub, style = cSty, color = cCol, textcolor = color.white, size = size.small)

//  contraWick: the closing-side wick, which on a sell is the LOWER one. Past oppWickX it
//  is demand showing up inside the very bar being sold, so the setup is downgraded.
contraWick = rng > 0 and (closeWick / rng) >= oppWickX

if buySig or sellSig
    warn = (not hasWick and not eorHere) or contraWick
    side = buySig ? "lower" : "upper"
    //  The orange label says WEAK outright rather than relying on the colour. Zee:
    //  "the orange label.. can it say WEAK BUY SETUP.. add the word WEAK on all orange
    //  ones". A colour has to be remembered; the word is read. It is orange because the
    //  law would stand down here - no wick and no end-of-market to justify the reaction.
    //  When the campaign's last candle was capped and THIS bar turned, the document has
    //  its own name for the whole thing, so the headline says that instead of the generic
    //  one - line 250: "ussko END OF RISING MARKET bolta hu.. SELL SETUP".
    headline = eorNow ? (buySig ? "END OF FALLING MARKET - BUY SETUP" : "END OF RISING MARKET - SELL SETUP") : (buySig ? "BUY SETUP" : "SELL SETUP")
    base = (warn ? "WEAK " : "") + headline
    flag = warn ? "  ⚠️" : ""
    line2 = side + " wick " + str.tostring(wickPct, "#") + "%  -  " + wickMeaning
    line3 = eorHere ? (buySig ? "\nend of falling market just before - the fall was already capped" : "\nend of rising market just before - the rise was already capped") : ""
    //  LAW LINE 156, the full definition: "aggressive selling: supply has hit + has a
    //  wick + closing strong bearish". All three present is the strongest reading the
    //  document offers, so it gets said in those words rather than left to be inferred.
    //  AGGRESSIVE SELLING, on the exact conditions Zee set out on 2026-09-20: "ONLY if an
    //  end of rising market occurs.. then if the reaction is bearish BUT IT HAS A LARGE
    //  VOLUME (almost equal to the candle before it).. then if the bearish reaction candle
    //  has a wick on top of it. then we can label it aggressive selling".
    //  That is line 113 - "because small blue candle has big volume (end of rising market
    //  type) + next bar with wick on top -> so means this big red volume is of AGGRESSIVE
    //  SELLING and not of demand" - and line 250 says the same.
    //  THE LOUD VOLUME IS THE POINT. A quiet reaction is already the ideal case and needs
    //  no rescuing; this names the OTHER case, where the reaction is as heavy as the bar
    //  before it and would look like demand without the capping and the wick. My previous
    //  version used line 156's wording (wick + strong close) and ignored the volume
    //  condition, so it was calling quiet reactions aggressive too.
    loudReact  = not quiet
    cappedBefore = eorNow or eorHere or cappedRun
    aggressive = loudReact and cappedBefore and hasWick
    line4 = aggressive ? (buySig ? "\nAGGRESSIVE BUYING - loud reaction, but capped before and wicked below" : "\nAGGRESSIVE SELLING - loud reaction, but capped before and wicked above") : (strongClose ? (buySig ? "\nstrong bullish closing" : "\nstrong bearish closing") : "")
    //  STRONG IMBALANCE - the reaction did not just qualify as quiet, it collapsed. The
    //  other side has stopped showing up, which is line 98's ideal case.
    volRatio = quietBase > 0 ? volume / quietBase * 100 : 100
    line5 = strongImb ? "\nSTRONG IMBALANCE 💯  reaction only " + str.tostring(volRatio, "#") + "% of the last campaign bar" : ""
    //  LINE 234: "2 bar setup + fake break of this support + forms a wick + closing again
    //  inside support (STRONGEST)". So when the level was pierced and taken back, the
    //  setup is not merely valid - the document calls it the strongest case it describes.
    line6 = fakeBreak ? (fakeReact ? "\nSTRONGER DUE TO FAKE BREAK - the reaction itself took the level back" : "\nSTRONGER DUE TO FAKE BREAK - the campaign pierced support and lost it") : ""
    line7 = engulfN >= 2 ? "\nStronger: engulfs last " + str.tostring(engulfN) : ""
    txt  = base + flag + "\n" + line2 + line3 + line4 + line5 + line6 + line7
    bCol = warn ? color.new(color.orange, 0) : (buySig ? color.new(color.teal, 0) : color.new(color.red, 0))
    bSty = buySig ? label.style_label_up : label.style_label_down
    label.new(bar_index, buySig ? low : high, txt, style = bSty, color = bCol, textcolor = color.white, size = size.normal)

// ---- WHY NOT? ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Only on bars that turned against a run of minRun or more - i.e. the ones that had a
//  chance. Shows the actual numbers behind every gate, so a bar that should have marked
//  can be argued about with figures rather than guessed at.
if showWhy and runLen >= minRun and not fired
    wPct  = rng > 0 ? wick / rng * 100 : 0
    vPct  = quietBase > 0 ? volume / quietBase * 100 : 0
    peakPct = volPeakN > 0 ? runPeak / volPeakN * 100 : 0
    reason = not isCampaign ? "campaign NOT heavy" : (not quiet and not wicked) ? "reaction neither quiet nor wicked" : (not quiet and wicked and not cappedRun) ? "loud + wicked, but NO capping in the campaign" : "?"
    wTxt = "no setup: " + reason
    wSub = "campaign " + str.tostring(runLen) + " bars, peak " + str.tostring(peakPct, "#") + "% of the " + str.tostring(lookN) + "-bar high (need " + str.tostring(pkX * 100, "#") + "%)" + "\nreaction vol " + str.tostring(vPct, "#") + "% of ref (quiet needs <=" + str.tostring(relX * 100, "#") + "%)" + "\nwick " + str.tostring(wPct, "#") + "% (needs " + str.tostring(wickX * 100, "#") + "%)   capped in campaign: " + (cappedRun ? "yes" : "no")
    label.new(bar_index, runDir < 0 ? low : high, wTxt + "\n" + wSub, style = runDir < 0 ? label.style_label_up : label.style_label_down, color = color.new(color.gray, 25), textcolor = color.white, size = size.tiny)

alertcondition(buySig,  "VSISA buy setup",  "VSISA: quiet reaction after a selling campaign")
alertcondition(sellSig, "VSISA sell setup", "VSISA: quiet reaction after a buying campaign")

// ---- the counter ------------------------------------------------------------------------
var int nRuns = 0, var int nCamp = 0, var int nAbs = 0, var int nFire = 0
var int nWick = 0, var int nDays = 0
if dir != 0 and runDir != 0 and dir != runDir and runLen >= minRun
    nRuns += 1
if isCampaign
    nCamp += 1
if absorbed
    nAbs += 1
    if not quiet
        nWick += 1
if fired
    nFire += 1
isNewDay = ta.change(time("D")) != 0
if isNewDay
    nDays += 1

if showTbl and barstate.islast
    var table t = table.new(position.top_right, 3, 7, border_width = 1)
    d = math.max(nDays, 1)
    table.cell(t, 0, 0, "VSISA funnel",  text_color = color.white, bgcolor = color.new(color.blue, 40))
    table.cell(t, 1, 0, "total",         text_color = color.white, bgcolor = color.new(color.blue, 40))
    table.cell(t, 2, 0, "per day",       text_color = color.white, bgcolor = color.new(color.blue, 40))
    table.cell(t, 0, 1, "runs of " + str.tostring(minRun) + "+ one way", text_color = color.gray)
    table.cell(t, 1, 1, str.tostring(nRuns),  text_color = color.gray)
    table.cell(t, 2, 1, str.tostring(nRuns / d, "#.##"), text_color = color.gray)
    table.cell(t, 0, 2, "heavy (campaign)",   text_color = color.silver)
    table.cell(t, 1, 2, str.tostring(nCamp),  text_color = color.silver)
    table.cell(t, 2, 2, str.tostring(nCamp / d, "#.##"), text_color = color.silver)
    table.cell(t, 0, 3, "low volume OR wick", text_color = color.yellow)
    table.cell(t, 1, 3, str.tostring(nAbs),   text_color = color.yellow)
    table.cell(t, 2, 3, str.tostring(nAbs / d, "#.##"), text_color = color.yellow)
    table.cell(t, 0, 4, "   of those, by WICK", text_color = color.orange)
    table.cell(t, 1, 4, str.tostring(nWick),  text_color = color.orange)
    table.cell(t, 2, 4, str.tostring(nWick / d, "#.##"), text_color = color.orange)
    table.cell(t, 0, 5, "CONFIRMED setups",   text_color = color.lime)
    table.cell(t, 1, 5, str.tostring(nFire),  text_color = color.lime)
    table.cell(t, 2, 5, str.tostring(nFire / d, "#.##"), text_color = color.lime)
    table.cell(t, 0, 6, "days counted",       text_color = color.gray)
    table.cell(t, 1, 6, str.tostring(nDays),  text_color = color.gray)
    table.cell(t, 2, 6, "", text_color = color.gray)
